From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] terminal: treat EINVAL from TIOCGPTPEER like ENOTTY

lxc_devpts_terminal() opens the pty slave with ioctl(TIOCGPTPEER). When the
ioctl is not available it expects ENOTTY, and falls back to opening the slave
by its pty number from the devpts instance, which it is allowed to do for the
container's own console and ttys. Any other errno is a hard failure.

On a Linux 3.4 kernel the ioctl does not exist and the pty master answers with
EINVAL instead:

  ioctl(40, TIOCSPTLCK, [0])         = 0
  ioctl(40, TIOCGPTPEER, 0x80102)    = -1 EINVAL

so lxc stops with

  terminal.c:lxc_devpts_terminal:998 - Invalid argument - Failed to allocate new pty device
  conf.c:lxc_setup_console:2123 - Failed to allocate console from container's devpts instance
  start.c:do_start:1275 - Failed to setup container "android"

and the Android container of the Halium image never starts.

Take the same fallback for EINVAL as for ENOTTY. Callers that require
TIOCGPTPEER still get ENODEV.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/lxc/terminal.c
+++ b/src/lxc/terminal.c
@@ -989,6 +989,11 @@
 	if (fd_pty < 0) {
 		switch (errno) {
 		case ENOTTY:
+		case EINVAL:
+			/*
+			 * Kernels without TIOCGPTPEER are expected to answer
+			 * ENOTTY, but the pty master on Linux 3.4 returns EINVAL.
+			 */
 			SYSTRACE("Pure fd-based terminal allocation not possible");
 			break;
 		case ENOSPC:
